The `test_zms_read_evil_ate` test case fails because a manually crafted "evil" ATE is considered valid by `zms_read`, even though its `metadata` field (which contains the ZMS magic number and version) is zeroed out.

The `zms_ate_is_valid` helper function, used by `zms_read` to determine if an ATE is well-formed, currently does not check the `metadata` field for non-header ATEs. This allows an ATE with an incorrect or missing ZMS magic number to be processed as a valid data entry.

The fix involves adding a check for the `ZMS_MAGIC_NUMBER` within `zms_ate_is_valid`. This ensures that any ATE, regardless of its ID, must contain the correct ZMS magic number in its `metadata` field to be considered valid. With this change, the "evil" ATE in the test (which has `metadata` set to 0) will be correctly identified as invalid, leading `zms_read` to ignore it and return `-ENOENT` as expected.

The `zms_ate_is_valid` function is typically defined as an `inline` function in `subsys/kvss/zms/zms_priv.h`.

